# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for barch.
#
# Use with:
#   TSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=ci/tsan.supp" ...
#
# See DONE 189 for how the one entry below was arrived at, and ci/README.md
# for the rest of the TSan recipe (the runtime has to be preloaded, and ASLR
# turned off with setarch -R, because _barch.so is dlopened by python).

# The two mutex suppressions that used to live here are gone - TODO 334.
#
# They hid "unlock of an unlocked mutex" on upgrade_write_mtx, and both blamed
# pthread_mutex_timedlock for being uninstrumented. That was the wrong call:
# `nm -D libtsan.so.2` has an interceptor for timedlock. The one it has none for
# is pthread_mutex_clocklock, which is what libstdc++ actually calls for
# try_lock_for/try_lock_until on a steady_clock deadline.
#
# With the right name it could be annotated instead of suppressed:
# debuggable_server_lock now wraps every timed acquire in
# __tsan_mutex_pre_lock/__tsan_mutex_post_lock, which says what the missing
# interceptor would have said. One chaos run at 17 shards, annotations off: 53
# unbalanced unlocks. Same run with them on: 0. The old count at 347 shards was
# 1043, and 1043 = 3*347+2 against 53 = 3*17+2 - one report per lock object,
# because TSan marks a mutex broken after the first and never mentions it again.
#
# Neither entry is needed now, and both were hiding a real unbalanced unlock if
# one ever appeared inside the lock, so they are better off out.

# The lock's own diagnostics, not barch. capture_writer_stack() fills
# diags.writer_frames with backtrace() while a timeout dump could be reading it,
# and note_writer() sets the thread id and timestamp beside it. Racy, and it
# does not matter: it is a best-effort snapshot for a log line.
#
# It is also not in a release build. This whole block is behind
# BARCH_LOCK_DEBUG, which CMakeLists turns on for Debug and RelWithDebInfo -
# and RelWithDebInfo is what a sanitizer build uses. So this is a race in the
# instrumentation rather than in the thing being instrumented. 13 reports in
# one short-set run. See DONE 203 and TODO 213.
race:debuggable_server_lock::capture_writer_stack
race:debuggable_server_lock::note_writer
race:debuggable_server_lock::append_writer_stack

# The LRU stamp against a bulk page copy. DONE 295 made the read path write -
# `set_lru` marks a leaf's flags byte when an LRU policy is on, under nothing
# stronger than the shared latch GET already holds. A reader that copies a whole
# page reads that byte while the stamp writes it.
#
# Harmless, and specifically rather than vaguely: the write is `lock orb` on one
# byte, so a copy sees the old value or the new one and never a mixture -
# sizeof/alignof/is_always_lock_free are asserted next to leaf::make_size to keep
# that true. Only `leaf_lru_flag` differs between the two values, so `deleted()`,
# `expired()`, `is_compressed()` and `large()` - which decides how the page walker
# reads key_len - are identical either way, and nothing that consumes a copied
# page reads the LRU bit. The stamp is also the only flag mutation taken under a
# shared latch: every other one is on a write path holding the unique latch,
# checked against each call site rather than assumed.
#
# Named on the writer, not on the readers, which is the change worth explaining.
#
# This used to name the copiers - `shard::page` and `shard::glob` - and that list
# had to grow every time another one reached the stamp. It did: the 17 shard
# default (TODO 339) raised per-shard contention enough that the TSan CI job went
# red on `logical_allocator::iterate_pages`, the walker the compression and
# defrag passes share, and adding that uncovered a fourth,
# `logical_allocator::get_page_buffer` from `shard::maintenance`. Nothing had been
# lost from this file in between - both original entries were still here - the
# stamp simply has more readers than anyone had enumerated.
#
# So it is named once, at the only thing that writes: `set_leaf_lru` and
# `leaf::set_lru` exist for the stamp and for nothing else, so this covers every
# copier, present and future, without hiding anything but the byte that was
# argued harmless above. `leaf::set_flag` is deliberately *not* named - the other
# flag mutations go through it on write paths holding the unique latch, and a race
# on one of those would be a real finding.
#
# TODO 306 item 5 is still the change that removes the need for any of this, by
# moving the stamp off the read path.
race:art::set_leaf_lru
race:art::leaf::set_lru
